=== Brief 01 lines 11-27 (show up to 200 lines) ===
11:## Investigation Brief 01 — Ledger Sourcing
12:
13:Committee ruling (binding for extract):
14:
15:Finance QA traced rerun drift to parsers that latched onto the wrong ledger
16:slice. For extractor output, treat only the markdown section whose heading
17:line is exactly `## Transaction Ledger` as authoritative. The archive also
18:contains a working-copy ledger and prose that mentions ledger headings; those
19:are investigative context only.
20:
21:Ignore any ledger block that includes a line `provisional: true` (case
22:insensitive). When the same transaction id appears in multiple non-provisional
23:ledger blocks, the last such block in file order is the base row for that id.
24:
25:Locate section boundaries with regular expressions anchored to whole heading
26:lines at line start. Do not locate sections with indexOf or substring search.
27:

=== Brief 02 lines 252-261 (show up to 200 lines) ===
252:## Investigation Brief 02 — Status Precedence
253:
254:Auditor directive (mandatory for extract):
255:
256:Interview notes with the compliance desk confirmed status fights between mail,
257:amendments, and corrections. Ledger rows provide the base status (lowercase on
258:output). Status precedence from lowest to highest is: pending, approved,
259:rejected, reversed. Email excerpts may change status only when the From line
260:contains `compliance@` anywhere in the address; ignore all other senders.
261:

=== Brief 03 lines 468-478 (show up to 200 lines) ===
468:## Investigation Brief 03 — Compliance Mail Timing
469:
470:Extract policy (authoritative):
471:
472:The March mail-room audit explained missing holds: agents applied compliance
473:messages without checking dispatch dates. When an excerpt includes
474:`sent: YYYY-MM-DD`, apply the status change only if sent is greater than or
475:equal to the ledger date for that transaction. When no `sent:` line is present,
476:apply the status change if precedence allows. Correction notices always apply
477:after ledger rows, meeting amendments, and mail excerpts have been merged.
478:

=== Brief 04 lines 685-695 (show up to 200 lines) ===
685:## Investigation Brief 04 — Correction Notice Precedence
686:
687:Controller memo (binding reconciliation rule):
688:
689:The corrections desk ships multiple notices per field. When several notices
690:target the same transaction and field, the notice with the lexicographically
691:greatest `effective` date wins. For each winning notice, `previous_value` in
692:`reconciliation_report.jsonl` is the field value immediately before that notice
693:applies (after ledger, amendments, and emails). Owner corrections override
694:both ledger owners and signed meeting amendments when applied in this step.
695:

=== Brief 05 lines 828-837 (show up to 200 lines) ===
828:## Investigation Brief 05 — Meeting Amendments and Owners
829:
830:Committee ruling (binding for extract):
831:
832:Committee minutes show owner churn from unsigned drafts. Ledger owner is the
833:default. Meeting note amendments under `#### Amendment for TXN-<uuid>` replace
834:owner when `signed: true` (case insensitive) and, when the amendment includes
835:`effective: YYYY-MM-DD`, only if that date is greater than or equal to the
836:ledger date for the transaction.
837:

=== Brief 06 lines 1058-1068 (show up to 200 lines) ===
1058:## Investigation Brief 06 — Effective Dates on Output
1059:
1060:Auditor directive (mandatory for extract):
1061:
1062:Auditors compared effective_date columns to correction paperwork. Start from the
1063:ledger `date` field (YYYY-MM-DD on output). When a winning correction notice
1064:sets field `date` or `status`, use that notice's `effective` date as
1065:`effective_date` only if that correction modified status or date; otherwise
1066:keep the ledger date. `amount_usd` is numeric from the ledger with two decimal
1067:places in CSV and as a JSON number.
1068:

=== Brief 07 lines 1201-1212 (show up to 200 lines) ===
1201:## Investigation Brief 07 — Exception Flags (Part I)
1202:
1203:Extract policy (authoritative):
1204:
1205:Exception coding review: `exception_reason` is null unless a code applies. Set
1206:`over_limit` when `amount_usd` strictly exceeds 10000. Set `compliance_hold`
1207:when final status is `rejected`, a qualifying `compliance@` email referenced
1208:that transaction, and that email block includes a `sent:` line. The sent-date
1209:comparison used for `compliance_hold` is defined in Investigation Brief 09
1210:(Mid-Year Amendment), not Brief 03. Multiple reasons join with semicolon in
1211:lexical order of the reason codes.
1212:

=== Brief 08 lines 1419-1439 (show up to 200 lines) ===
1419:## Investigation Brief 08 — Exception Flags (Part II) and Artifacts
1420:
1421:Controller memo (binding reconciliation rule):
1422:
1423:Continued from Brief 07. Set `policy_waiver` when final status is `approved`,
1424:`amount_usd` > 10000, and a Policy Exception block exists for that transaction
1425:with `approved_by: compliance` on its own line. Set `retroactive_review` when
1426:final status is `reversed`, `amount_usd` > 5000, and a winning correction
1427:notice changed status for that transaction.
1428:
1429:`extract --outdir` must write: `transactions.json` as `{"items":[...]}` sorted
1430:by `transaction_id` ascending with fields transaction_id, owner, status
1431:(lowercase), effective_date, amount_usd, exception_reason (null or
1432:semicolon-joined codes). `transactions.csv` uses header
1433:transaction_id,owner,status,effective_date,amount_usd,exception_reason with the
1434:same rows; leave exception_reason blank when null. `exceptions.json` is
1435:`{"items":[...]}` with only rows where exception_reason is set.
1436:`reconciliation_report.jsonl` is one JSON object per winning correction with
1437:notice_id, transaction_id, field, previous_value, new_value, effective, sorted
1438:by notice_id ascending.
1439:

=== Brief 09 lines 1670-1681 (show up to 200 lines) ===
1670:## Investigation Brief 09 — Mid-Year Amendment
1671:
1672:Committee ruling (binding for extract):
1673:
1674:Mid-year QA found compliance holds disappearing after date corrections. This
1675:amendment supersedes any earlier wording about hold timing. For `compliance_hold`
1676:only, compare each qualifying email's `sent:` date against the transaction's
1677:ledger date immediately before correction notices are applied — that is, the date
1678:in effect after ledger load, meeting amendments, and compliance mail merges, but
1679:before any correction notice changes `date` or `status`. Brief 03 still governs
1680:mail-driven status changes; this amendment governs only the hold flag.
1681:
