252:## Investigation Brief 02 — Status Precedence
253:
254:Auditor directive (mandatory for extract):
255:
256:Interview notes with the compliance desk confirmed status fights between mail,
257:amendments, and corrections. Ledger rows provide the base status (lowercase on
258:output). Status precedence from lowest to highest is: pending, approved,
259:rejected, reversed. Email excerpts may change status only when the From line
260:contains `compliance@` anywhere in the address; ignore all other senders.
261:
262:## Investigation Brief 02 — Status Precedence — field notes
263:
264:Risk assessment WP-2138 ties capital project capitalization to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.
265:
266:Working paper WP-3210 documents a three-way match failure tied to capital project capitalization. The team escalated when repeated extractor runs produced different status columns for the same transaction id.
267:
268:Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.
269:
270:Working paper WP-6073 documents a three-way match failure tied to capital project capitalization. The team escalated when repeated extractor runs produced different status columns for the same transaction id.
271:
272:Lead reviewer J. Huang noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.

